# Page metadata

Design proposal, 2026-10-17. [brief.md](brief.md) remains the source of truth for owner decisions. This document makes implementation decisions for [issues #233, #240, #247, and #251](issues.md); assumptions beyond the brief are listed at the end. It specifies behavior, not an implemented feature.

## The decision

Tessel owns the outer document and its browser metadata. The embedded HTML supplies the page's identity through ordinary HTML tags, with useful defaults when tags are missing. Publishing one HTML file remains sufficient: no manifest, screenshot upload, or separate metadata form is required.

Public and unlisted pages have page-specific tabs and rich link previews, including an automatically generated image. Private pages expose only Tessel branding to link expanders and unauthenticated visitors. An authorized member sees the private page's own tab title and icon after access is verified; this does not change the private page's social metadata.

Producing metadata never runs the live page with live write access. A crawler reading metadata does not have to execute JavaScript to obtain a complete card.

## Visibility contract

| Surface | Public | Unlisted | Private |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Who can read the page | Anyone | Anyone holding the link | Signed-in invited members only |
| Initial outer title and icon | Page title and icon | Page title and icon | `Tessel` and Tessel icon, for every request |
| Tab after authorized page load | Page identity; may update from runtime | Page identity; may update from runtime | Page identity, set locally after membership verification |
| Description and social card | Page-specific | Page-specific | Generic Tessel text and brand image only |
| Preview image | Safe page screenshot, authored image, or generated card | Same as public | Fixed Tessel brand card; no content-derived image |
| Search | Indexable; canonical URL; eligible for sitemap | `noindex, nofollow, noarchive`; absent from sitemap and discovery lists | `noindex, nofollow, noarchive`; absent from sitemap and discovery lists |

Unlisted means link-accessible, not confidential. Its title, description, icon, and image can be fetched without signing in and copied by the app receiving the link. Show that explanation beside the visibility choice. Indexing directives are a discovery request, not an access gate. Do not block the unlisted route in `robots.txt`: crawlers need to read its `noindex` directive, and link expanders need its tags.

For denied, nonexistent, deleted, or withdrawn pages, return the same content-free metadata family. Do not distinguish a private resource from a nonexistent one by revealing its title, owner, icon, thumbnail, first paragraph, or access list. Authorized access can have a specific in-app screen, but social tags remain generic. Membership must be checked before delivering private HTML, metadata JSON, icons, images, or source files.

## Identity from one HTML file

Parse the submitted HTML with an HTML parser at each published revision, without executing scripts. Store a normalized metadata snapshot tied to that revision; the source HTML remains the authority for authored values. Do not derive metadata from a viewer's saved state, draft, cookies, URL query, or account.

Resolve each field independently in the order below. Use the first valid nonempty candidate; for duplicate tags of the same kind use the first valid one in document order.

| Field | Precedence |
| --- | --- |
| Subject | `<title>`, then `meta[property="og:title"]`, then first nonempty `<h1>`, then `Untitled page` |
| Description | `meta[name="description"]`, then `meta[property="og:description"]`, then the first meaningful visible paragraph from the safe snapshot, then `An interactive page on Tessel.` |
| Icon | Valid `<link rel="icon">` candidate, then Tessel icon |
| Image | Valid authored `og:image`, then safe rendered screenshot, then a generated page card |

The title is the author's deliberate tab name; a heading is a fallback. Tessel uses the same resolved subject for the tab, social title, generated card, and server-rendered fallback heading. Do not let a separate Open Graph title create a second identity when `<title>` exists. A description need not be unique if no meaningful content exists; never invent claims about an empty page.

Trim and collapse whitespace, remove control characters, and treat all extracted strings as text. Escape for each output context; never interpolate HTML into the outer head or an image template. Bound subjects to 120 Unicode characters and descriptions to 240, truncating at a word boundary where possible. These are proposed product limits, not platform guarantees. Show the resolved values and any truncation in the publisher's preview.

Format titles as `<subject> · Tessel`, adding the suffix once when the supplied title already ends with that exact suffix. The generic private/error title is simply `Tessel`.

Relative asset references resolve against the published HTML's asset base, never the publisher's browser filesystem or an arbitrary request host. Support inline image data and published assets so a single-file page can include its own icon. For multiple icons, prefer a valid square SVG source, then the highest-resolution square raster candidate. Sanitize/rasterize accepted sources in isolation and serve PNG icon variants at 32, 48, and 180 pixels from Tessel. Do not inject uploaded SVG markup into the wrapper. Tessel's fallback variants derive from its canonical mark.

Fetch remote authored icons/images only through a bounded ingestion service: HTTPS, no credentials, reject loopback/private/link-local destinations including after DNS resolution and each redirect, and cap redirects, bytes, decoded pixels, and processing time. Proposed limits are three redirects, 5 MB, 20 megapixels, and ten seconds. Re-encode images to strip active content and metadata. A missing, malformed, blocked, or timed-out asset falls through to the next choice without preventing publication. Never hotlink arbitrary author URLs from the wrapper head.

## The wrapper and the embedded page

The iframe's `<title>` and favicon do not supply the outer document's identity. The wrapper sets its own title and icon from the published snapshot. Its toolbar retains Tessel branding while the tab leads with the page.

For pages that assign `document.title` or change their icon in JavaScript, the injected Tessel bridge observes these values and reports updates to the wrapper. Accept messages only from the active iframe window, using a per-load channel nonce and a validated, bounded payload. Validate origin where the sandbox exposes one; an opaque sandbox origin alone cannot identify a sender. The bridge cannot set visibility, canonical URLs, social tags, or arbitrary outer head markup. Icon changes use the same safe asset path, never direct HTML injection.

Live changes affect the current viewer's tab only. They do not overwrite the published metadata snapshot: a personalized score or poll result must not become everyone's preview. For a page whose initial title exists only after JavaScript, capture the initial title during the safe publish render and use it when no authored static title/OG title exists, ahead of the heading fallback. Authors should put a stable `<title>` in the HTML for deterministic sharing.

On navigation, replace the previous title and icon immediately with the destination's available snapshot or generic Tessel defaults. Ignore late bridge messages and failed icon loads from the previous frame. Maintain one wrapper head owner so client navigation or framework head updates cannot restore stale metadata. On logout, membership revocation, or a transition to private without current access, clear private tab identity and remove the iframe. Check this on history restoration and tab resume as well as initial navigation.

Private initial HTML, hydration data, redirect/login responses, JSON-LD, and HTTP headers contain no page-derived identity, even for a member request. After authorization, the private client fetches its identity through a non-cacheable authorized endpoint and updates only the local tab. Private icons are also authorized and non-cacheable; fetch their bytes into a local blob URL for the tab and revoke it when leaving. Do not turn a private image into a publicly fetchable signed URL for favicon convenience.

## Link preview HTML

The canonical outer URL returns complete tags in its initial HTML, using the published snapshot without executing the embedded page. Use this behavior for all user agents; crawler detection must not be the security boundary.

For public and unlisted pages emit:

- `<title>`, description, canonical link, and icon links.
- `og:type=website`, `og:site_name=Tessel`, `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:url`, `og:image`, image type, width, height, and meaningful image alt text.
- `twitter:card=summary_large_image`, title, description, image, and image alt text with the same values.

Resolve canonical and asset URLs to absolute HTTPS URLs on trusted configured Tessel origins. Ignore tracking/query parameters and fragments when choosing identity. Do not reflect an untrusted Host header or author-supplied canonical/OG URL. Keep existing canonical page addresses; metadata introduces no second share route. Use opaque resource IDs rather than private titles in addresses and asset names.

For private and unavailable pages use title `Tessel`, description `Create and share interactive pages on Tessel.`, the Tessel icon, and a fixed Tessel brand card with generic alt text. Omit content-specific canonical/OG URLs and all content-derived structured data. Apply indexing directives in HTML and `X-Robots-Tag`. Do not redirect a link expander to a content-bearing special preview route.

Public pages also expose a safe heading and descriptive text in the outer body's initial HTML so a JavaScript-only embed has a readable indexable summary. This is a static, escaped summary from the same snapshot, not execution of author HTML in Tessel's origin. If the page has no meaningful description yet, omit it from the sitemap until a safe summary or authored description exists; the direct URL and card still work. Unlisted pages must never enter sitemaps or discovery feeds.

## Preview images and safe rendering

Always provide an image. Generate a 1200 × 630 PNG page card immediately from the resolved subject and description, with the canonical Tessel mark and product name. This is a useful page-specific fallback while rendering is pending, and the final fallback if rendering fails. A valid authored image is resized/padded into the same canvas without distorting it. No AI-generated image is needed.

If no authored image is available, render the published revision in a disposable browser at a fixed 1200 × 630 viewport. Capture the embedded page area, excluding Tessel's toolbar. Wait for a bounded initial readiness period, including fonts, then capture without clicking, scrolling, submitting forms, or granting permissions. Cap the entire run at ten seconds; do not wait indefinitely for network idle. Disable animations for capture. A blank output, loading/error-only result, crash, or deadline uses the generated card. Authors can inspect the resulting crop and supply `og:image` in their HTML if they want different art.

The renderer is a distinct execution mode, not a logged-out visit to the live page:

- Supply only the immutable published HTML/assets and a frozen, non-sensitive initial runtime snapshot. Start with empty cookies/storage, no member session, no API keys, and no ambient infrastructure credentials.
- Replace the Tessel runtime with a preview adapter. Reads use the frozen snapshot or empty defaults. Every mutation, including votes, counters, uploads, analytics events, and background jobs, is rejected with a stable preview-mode result. Authorization at the backend also refuses mutations from a preview principal; hiding methods in the JavaScript SDK is insufficient.
- Block outbound networking by default at the browser/container boundary, including fetch, beacons, forms, navigation, WebSockets, service workers, and raw network access. Allow only read access to a narrowly scoped asset snapshot service. Do not allow live application APIs, third-party endpoints, or arbitrary URLs just because a request uses GET. Any remote dependencies to be supported must first be copied into the validated asset snapshot; otherwise the screenshot falls back to the generated card.
- Run hostile HTML in an isolated sandbox with CPU, memory, request, byte, and time limits; disable downloads, popups, and access to local/internal services. Destroy the environment after the job.

This permits JavaScript-built content while ensuring an automatic on-load vote cannot reach production. Pages that need unavailable external scripts/data still get complete text tags and a generated image. Private pages get no automated screenshot or public content-derived card at all.

Metadata, image, icon, and GET/HEAD page handlers are read-only: a paste must not increment a page view or vote. A preview renderer that executes the wrapper must still lack all mutation authority. Live-page code should commit a vote only after a deliberate interaction; passive reads must not write. These measures stop Tessel preview generation from voting, but cannot prove that every arbitrary public browser is human. An external service that executes a public page as a normal visitor remains a reason to require explicit actions and backend abuse controls for live mutations.

## Publishing, storage, and revocation

Publishing atomically records the revision, extracted metadata, visibility epoch, and generated fallback reference, then queues at most one render job for that revision. The database owns permissions, metadata snapshots, job state, and asset references; object storage holds image bytes. Expensive rendering runs at publish time, never in response to a crawler fetching a URL. Repeated publish events deduplicate by page/revision/epoch and renderer version.

Expose coarse publisher states: `Preparing preview`, `Ready`, or `Using generated preview`, with a `Retry preview` action on failure. A render failure does not fail the page publication. Do not automatically retry expensive failed renders. A newer revision supersedes an older job; recheck page liveness, owner, revision, visibility, and epoch before rendering and again before attaching any result. Record pending object keys before writes so cleanup can find abandoned uploads.

Serve images and icons through a Tessel access-controlled delivery route; do not expose the backing bucket or a redirect to a durable public object URL. Use opaque asset IDs tied to page, revision, and sharing epoch. Every request, including historical image/icon URLs and conditional requests, checks current visibility and asset eligibility before any cached bytes or 304 response are released. Missing/deleted/private assets return a generic image or content-free denial, never old page bytes. Previously shared images are not exempt because their URL is known.

Start with `Cache-Control: no-store` for page responses and page-derived asset responses, and disable shared edge caching on these routes. Internal metadata/image caches may use revision/epoch keys but sit behind the current access check; fail closed if visibility cannot be established. A later CDN optimization must preserve that gate on every hit. The fixed generic Tessel brand assets can be cached publicly.

On public/unlisted → private, deletion, or withdrawal, the visibility mutation immediately revokes public access to the source, metadata, icons, and every image revision. Advance the epoch, invalidate internal delivery references, cancel obsolete jobs, remove discovery entries, and schedule byte cleanup. Do not serve stale metadata while revalidating access. Private → public/unlisted creates a fresh epoch and preview only from the revision deliberately published under that visibility. Public → unlisted removes indexing/discovery and updates robots immediately; its rich card remains allowed.

Tessel can stop future delivery but cannot erase cards, browser history, screenshots, or downloaded images already retained by recipients and third-party caches. Explain this beside visibility changes. Revisioned image URLs help fresh fetches see edits; chat tools may retain a card keyed by the page URL until their refresh mechanism runs. Do not promise immediate external updates or confidentiality retroactively.

## Acceptance checks

| Issue / scenario | Required evidence before shipping |
| --- | --- |
| #233: private title leak | Paste a private “Q3 layoffs plan” URL into a chat signed out: only Tessel branding. Inspect initial HTML, hydration, headers, images, icon routes, and nonmember requests for absence of the title and paragraph. A member sees the real tab identity only after authorization. |
| #240: wrapper ignores identity | Publish one HTML file containing a title and data-URL favicon. Reload and navigate client-side: the outer tab uses both. Test missing/malformed values, runtime updates, frame replacement, and logout reset. |
| #247: JavaScript-only page | Fetch the outer URL without JavaScript: complete OG and Twitter fields, meaningful title, and a reachable image. Confirm safe JavaScript rendering can produce a screenshot; blocked dependencies/timeouts still yield a page-specific generated card. Paste public and unlisted links into a real chat/social tool. |
| #251: phantom votes | Record live vote count and write audit events, then publish/render, fetch GET/HEAD metadata/assets repeatedly, and paste links. Counts and writes stay unchanged. Test malicious on-load SDK writes, direct API calls, beacons, forms, and GET-based side effects; each is blocked in preview execution. A deliberate live vote still works. |
| Visibility and asset races | Warm caches and retain all old icon/image/source URLs; make the page private or delete it while rendering is in flight. Fresh requests, including conditional requests, yield no old bytes, and the old job cannot reattach them. |
| Search and failures | Public summary/canonical are readable without scripts; unlisted/private robots headers and tags are correct and no sitemap entries exist. Broken images, unsafe remote URLs, injected metadata, blank captures, and failed jobs produce bounded safe fallbacks. |

These are implementation acceptance checks, not claims of tests already run for this documentation change.

## Assumptions made while the owner is away

- A stable outer page URL, published revision, membership gate, and isolated embed are available or will be built. No app implementation exists in this checkout beyond the monorepo scaffold, so service and endpoint descriptions above are contracts rather than observations about existing code.
- HTML is the only required authoring interface. Optional preview settings and image uploads are deferred; standard tags cover explicit control without contradicting the single-file brief.
- An authorized member may see private identity in their own tab. Generic server metadata and client-only private tab updates intentionally separate that local display from link previews. Tab names may still appear in that member's browser history or screen sharing.
- Unlisted pages consent to rich previews when their link is shared. They are not listed internally for anonymous discovery, and their URL alone grants read access, as the brief specifies.
- Automatic screenshot capture is desirable when safe, but the generated card is sufficient when page dependencies cannot be safely rendered. Rendering must not use live saved user data; adding an author-selected public data snapshot would require a separate explicit contract.
- The separator, field/asset limits, image dimensions, capture deadline, state labels, and conservative cache policy are initial design choices. They can be tuned without changing the privacy or mutation boundaries.
- This proposal changes neither live visibility nor infrastructure. It does not require choosing a new domain, storage vendor, rendering vendor, or framework to settle the product behavior.
